From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: macOS's own shortcuts go to the VM while it has the keyboard

Applies after all other Cocoa patches and omacvm-cocoa-shortcuts-logic.patch
(the header with the logic; last in build-qemu-gpu-runtime.sh).

What: while the VM window has the keyboard (full grab on, our app in front,
our window key), macOS's global shortcuts are switched off with
CGSSetGlobalHotKeyOperatingMode (as VirtualBox and UTM do), and on again the
moment it has not. Screenshots (Cmd+Shift+3/4/5 and the Ctrl variants),
Mission Control, App Expose, Show Desktop, Launchpad, Ctrl+arrows,
Spotlight, input sources, Cmd+Tab, Cmd+`, Quick Note, Force Quit's chord and
the rest of macOS's list then reach the VM like any other key. Apple's own
top-row key codes (Mission Control, Spotlight, Dictation, Do Not Disturb,
Launchpad, Dashboard) reach the VM as the F-key they sit on.

Why: the HID event tap from full grab sees most chords, but not all of them
(macOS handles some before any tap, and the tap needs Accessibility), so
Cmd+Shift+4 or Cmd+F11 could still go to macOS.

Safety: the switch belongs to this process's window-server connection. When
QEMU exits or crashes, macOS turns the shortcuts back on by itself (checked:
a process that switched them off and was killed leaves them on); another
process cannot keep them off. A watchdog thread turns them on when the
window's thread has not answered for 2 s, so a hung VM window never keeps
them. OMACVM_MAC_SHORTCUTS=1 leaves them with macOS. The escape combo
(Ctrl+Option+Cmd+Esc) is OmacVM Gestures'; when Gestures is not running the
VM sees it, and in full screen it then hands the keyboard back to macOS
(shortcuts on, app hidden). Media keys are not shortcuts: the Bridge routes
them as before.

Upstream: not proposed (QEMU's Cocoa UI has no keyboard-grab policy of this
kind; it would need an option there).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/keys/test-shortcuts.sh (the logic in
ui/omacvm-shortcuts.h against macOS's whole shortcut list) and
src/tests/vm-shortcuts.sh (each chord reaches a real QEMU's input layer).

